Z||000000||maryjane||09-07-2001||07:28 PM||||What usually triggers a "this program has performed an illegal operation and will be shut down" error? I've occasionally gotten them while offline trying to do several things at once(say, printing or scanning and running another program in background), but I recently got one while visiting a DOD site, Can the visit to the site cause the error or is it just a coincidence?<P><BR>I want to die in my sleep just like my Grandpa, not screaming and yelling like his passengers.||||reg||5
Z||000001||Raydar||09-07-2001||07:33 PM||||Prolly just a coincidence.<BR>I've noticed that some web pages with animation (Java or Flash?) will cause my computer to freeze solid. Have to punch the reset to restart it.<BR>And that's with W2K.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_wtf.gif"> (Supposedly crash resistant.)<P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"<BR></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000002||thomas_l||09-07-2001||07:39 PM||||That error is usually caused by a program that either tries to grab system memory or has something stored in memory that is corrupt. The first steps ought to be some system maintenance and clean-up. Scan disk (thorough) and defrag. If you feel like spending some money, Norton System Utilities has some tools to help you track down and maybe fix some of those types of issues.||||reg||1
Z||000003||isthiswhereiputausername?||09-07-2001||07:47 PM||||click details... get the error..this program has caused an illegal op in _________ <P>that will tell us what program is conflicting..theres fixes for everyone.<P>what operating system? win98?||||reg||1
Z||000004||maryjane||09-07-2001||07:56 PM||||w95 on that computer- Ran scandisk and defrag<BR>this am. Program causing the error was IE browser. No biggie as long as it doesn't continue to happen. Just never had it happen online. Thanks<BR>Don||||reg||1
Z||000005||theogre||09-08-2001||05:02 PM||||There may be nothing actually wrong with the machine... <P>The key words here.... "trying to do several things at once(say, printing or scanning and running another program in background)"<P>This often means there is a resource problem. Everyone is familliar with RAM and Disk space but they don't know that these are deivided into diferant regions for different uses.<P>These regions, aka resources, contain things like "stack" memeory. Stacks keep track of data moving between programs and where(and how big) all the windows on your desktop are.<P>In Win 95, 98, and 98SE, you can usually help or cure a resource problem by doing the following. (The settings that follow have been tested on hundreds of win9x machines.)<P>Right Click "My Computer" and open Properties. (Or open "system" in "Control Panel.")<P>Under the performance tab, click File System<P>All systems... Set "Typical Roll" to "Network Server." Do this even if it's a laptop. Ignore the words of the name. Just set all Win9x machines to Network Server. This tells windows to create more stack space for background tasks like AntiVirus and Printing. (Yes this take a bit of RAM but the improvment to system stability is worth it.)<P>Win98 and 98SE... Under file system's floppy disk tab, clear the check from "search for new floppy." This makes the machine boot faster. (95 doesn't have this tab)<P>click ok to close file system.<BR>now click "virtual memory"<P>Click "let me specify"<BR>Type 128 into the "minimum" box. leave max alone.<BR>Click OK, and Click yes to the warning box that will pop up. (This setting reduces windows constantly resizing the swap file but allows it to grow beyond 128MB if needed.)<P>click ok again and reboot.<P>If it still acts up.... remove you printer driver (right ckick the printer and then click delete) then reboot and reinstall the printer.<P>Also, If it's an HP printer, see if there is a driver on HP's web site (www.hp.com) Those driver are usually more up to date then the one that came in the printer box. (Same for most printer really....)<P>Windows applications always use the printer driver even if you aren't printing. They have to ask the driver for information like margin and default paper size so they can correctly display your douments on screen.<P>------------------<BR><I><B>Screamin Yellow Zonkers... If it's Screamin and Yellow, I ain't eatin it.</B></I><p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 09-08-2001).]||||reg||1
